
  This week, we compared the TCS34725 colour sensor with the AS7265x spectral triad. The data from the TCS34725 was very unstable 
and incapable of consistently providing the data we needed, often only detecting red reliably. On the other hand, the AS7265x 
provided a much more accurate dataset. Instead of outputting a single colour value like the TCS34725, the spectral sensor provides
18 different channels representing light intensity across various wavelengths.

  My work this week focused on understanding what each wavelength represents and how to use them in the project. For our needs,
we do not require extremely accurate colour data; instead, we will focus on 5 specific channels: blue (460 nm and 485 nm), green (560 nm), 
and red (610 nm and 645 nm). By determining which of these channels stands out the most, we can easily identify the scanned colour.
Once we test the sensor with different objects next week, we will start mapping these data values to different parts of an audio wave. 
We are currently using our own samples, but once Nadia sends the sample she mentioned, we will analyze it and integrate it into our final project.
